I am pretty sure this isn't ever going to happen, honestly.  Consider
the skillset required, and how that skillset becomes more rare every
year that passes -- you have to be able to read and function in pre-ANSI
C, and you also have to be fluent in a modern language.  (C++ is
probably not considered 'modern' anymore, though it will be 'current'
for quite some time yet.)

Now consider how few people even have the first half of that skillset,
and how many of those are interested in learning the second half.  Then
consider how many of those people play Nethack, and how many of THOSE
people would ever be interested in tinkering.

Then filter it out one last time by the subset of those people who are
sufficiently competent to both start and complete such a project.  See
that last guy sitting there?  Yeah, he's busy.

